## Test Smell Audit — `CatalogTest.java`

**Framework:** JUnit 5 · **Language:** Java · **Production class:** `Catalog.java`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

1 test (`addThrowsOnNegativeCount`) is clean. 1 test (`quantityZeroOnUnknownSku`) is itself clean but affected by a class-level smell.

---

### Findings by Severity

#### HIGH

**① Conditional Test Logic** — `addConditionalLogic` (line 26)

```java
if (sku.equals("b")) {
    catalog.add(sku, 10);
} else {
    catalog.add(sku, 1);
}
```

The `if/else` inside the loop means the reader must mentally execute the branch to understand what's being set up. The single aggregate assertion (`assertEquals(12, ...)`) compounds the problem — if it fails, you don't know which branch is wrong.

**Fix:** Use separate, explicit `add` calls or `@ParameterizedTest`:

```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesQuantityAcrossSkus() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

---

**② Mystery Guest** — `addLoadsFromFile` (line 40)

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
```

The test round-trips through the filesystem — `Files.createTempFile`, `writeString`, `readString`. A filesystem failure will masquerade as a test failure. The data is entirely self-contained, so the file serves no purpose.

**Fix:** Inline the test data:

```java
@Test
void addMakesSkuInStock() {
    catalog.add("x", 7);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

---

**③ Sleepy Test** — `addSleepy` (line 50)

```java
Thread.sleep(200);
```

`Catalog` is synchronous and in-memory. The 200 ms sleep adds latency to every run with zero benefit.

**Fix:** Remove the sleep entirely:

```java
@Test
void addSetsQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

**④ Assertion-Free Test** — `addAndDoNothing` (line 58)

```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    // no assertion
}
```

This test will pass even if `add` silently drops data, corrupts state, or becomes a no-op. It provides false coverage confidence.

**Fix:** Assert the expected side effect:

```java
@Test
void addStoresQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

#### MEDIUM

**⑤ Eager Test** — `doEverythingAtOnce` (line 64)

Calls **4 distinct** production methods: `add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`. When the assertion fails, any of the four could be responsible.

**Fix:** Split into focused tests — one per behavior:

```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesPerSku() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 2);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(2, catalog.quantity("b"));
}
```

---

**⑥ Magic Number Test** — `quantityAfterAdds` (line 77)

```java
catalog.add("a", 41);
catalog.add("a", 1);
assertEquals(42, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

Why 41 + 1 = 42? The values are arbitrary and don't communicate intent. A reader must reverse-engineer the arithmetic.

**Fix:** Use intention-revealing constants:

```java
@Test
void addMergesQuantityForSameSku() {
    int firstBatch = 5;
    int secondBatch = 3;
    catalog.add("a", firstBatch);
    catalog.add("a", secondBatch);
    assertEquals(firstBatch + secondBatch, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

