**⑦ Sensitive Equality** — `toStringFormat` (line 85)

```java
assertEquals("Catalog(items=1)", catalog.toString());
```

This locks the test to the exact `toString` format. Any cosmetic change (e.g., `Catalog{items=1}`) breaks the test even though behavior is correct.

**Fix:** Assert on the meaningful property instead:

```java
@Test
void toStringContainsItemCount() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertTrue(catalog.toString().contains("1"));
}
```

Or, if `toString` format truly matters as a contract, document that intent explicitly in the test name and add a comment.

---

**⑧ Exception Handling in Tests** — `addNullSkuManualCatch` (line 92)

```java
try {
    catalog.add(null, 1);
    fail("should have thrown");
} catch (Exception ex) {
    assertNotNull(ex.getMessage());
}
```

Manual `try/catch` + `fail` is fragile — catching the broad `Exception` type could swallow unexpected errors. The companion test `addThrowsOnNegativeCount` already demonstrates the correct pattern.

**Fix:** Use `assertThrows`:

```java
@Test
void addNullSkuThrows() {
    IllegalArgumentException ex = assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> catalog.add(null, 1));
    assertTrue(ex.getMessage().contains("sku is required"));
}
```

